excludes `.git`, `node_modules`, and `intermediates`, but not `docs/`. After migration, this would copy the entire docs workspace (potentially including `docs/node_modules/`, `docs/.next/`) into the test snapshot before the sync script strips it.\n\nI verified the code at line 80 and confirmed the issue. The outcome (docs gets stripped by the sync script later) is correct, but the performance impact is real and the pattern is inconsistent — `intermediates/` is already excluded from the rsync even though it too would be stripped later.\n\n**Change made**: Added new Step 3.5 to add `--exclude='docs'` to the rsync command. This is consistent with the existing `intermediates` exclusion pattern.\n\n### 1.3 tsconfig.json include picks up docs files (Accepted — critical)\n\nThis was the most important finding in the review. The root `tsconfig.json` has `\"include\": [\"**/*.ts\", \"**/*.tsx\"]` with an exclude list that does not contain `docs/**/*`. After migration, `bun run tc` (which runs `tsc --noEmit`) would attempt to type-check docs files under incompatible compiler options:\n- Runtime: `moduleResolution: \"node\"`, `module: \"ES2022\"`, `strict: true`, no `jsx`\n- Docs: `moduleResolution: \"bundler\"`, `module: \"esnext\"`, `strict: false`, `jsx: \"preserve\"`\n\nI verified both tsconfig files directly. The incompatibility is clear-cut and would cause a build failure in CI.\n\n**Change made**: Added new Step 2.1 (now the first step in Phase 2) to add `\"docs/**/*\"` to the root tsconfig's exclude array. Also added cross-references in the verification section (Step 6.1) and edge cases list (item 7). Added the file to the code references table and summary of changes table.\n\nI did not disagree with this finding at all. It was a genuine gap in the original plan — the plan mentioned keeping separate tsconfigs but never addressed the root tsconfig's broad include glob.\n\n### 1.4 / 3.1 release.ts schema handling for docs (Accepted)\n\nThe review identified two related gaps in `release.ts`:\n1. The validation phase reverts `schemas/` but not `docs/public/schemas/` after test schema generation.\n2. The execute phase stages `package.json CHANGELOG.md schemas/` but not `docs/public/schemas/`.\n\nI verified both at the actual line numbers (`release.ts:436` and `release.ts:510`). The review is correct on both counts. After migration, `generate-schemas` writes to both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`, so both locations need to be handled.\n\n**Change made**: Added new Step 3.4 covering both the validation revert extension and the execute `git add` extension, with code snippets showing the exact changes needed.\n\n### 1.5 SOP line 40 package.json transform description (Accepted)\n\nThe review noted that the SOP's prose sentence at line 40 describing the `package.json` transformation does not mention `workspaces` removal. The plan's Step 5.2 added `workspaces` to the strip-list section but didn't address this separate prose sentence.\n\nI verified the SOP at line 40 and confirmed it only mentions name, publishConfig, homepage, and release scripts.\n\n**Change made**: Added explicit instruction in Step 5.2 to also update the SOP line 40 sentence.\n\n---\n\n## Gaps Addressed\n\n### 2.1 Vercel Ignored Build Step shallow clone (Addressed with caveat)\n\nThe review raised a concern that `git diff --quiet HEAD^ HEAD -- docs/` might fail in Vercel's shallow clones. This is a valid concern.\n\nHowever, after thinking about it, the failure mode is safe: if `HEAD^` is unavailable, `git diff` exits non-zero, which Vercel interprets as \"proceed with build.\" The worst case is an unnecessary build, not a missed build. I added a caveat to Step 7.3 explaining this and noting the more robust `VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA` alternative if it becomes a problem.\n\nI did not change the recommended approach because the simple `git diff` command is more standard and the failure mode is acceptable.\n\n### 2.3 package.json field ordering (Addressed)\n\nThe review noted the plan didn't specify where in the field order to place `\"workspaces\"`. I updated Step 1.2 to recommend placing it after `\"engines\"` and before `\"scripts\"`, adjacent to other structural metadata. This is a minor readability concern but worth being precise about in a plan that aims to be standalone.\n\n### 2.4 docsAvailable comment (Addressed)\n\nThe review suggested updating the comment on the `docsAvailable` guard since it will now always be true. The plan already said \"keep the check for defensive safety,\" which I agree with. I expanded the text in Step 2.2 to recommend updating the comment at line 75 and to note the silent-failure behavior.\n\n### 2.5 release.ts startsWith logic (Addressed)\n\nThe review confirmed this works correctly but suggested adding an explicit verification note. I added a paragraph after Step 3.3 confirming the `startsWith` check works with the `\"docs/\"` entry against Git's relative path output.\n\n---\n\n## Observations Incorporated\n\n### 3.3 Biome scripts outside scope (Noted)\n\nThe review noted that `scripts/` is not in Biome's `includes` list, so modifications to scripts won't be linted. I added this as edge case item 8, since it's useful context for the implementing agent.\n\n### 3.4 Smoke test docs/public/schemas dependency (Fixed)\n\nThe proposed smoke test checked `docs/public/schemas/` existence, but this directory is only created by `generate-schemas` and won't exist on a fresh checkout. I changed the test to check `docs/public/` instead and added a note explaining why.\n\n---\n\n## Things I Disagreed With or Didn't Change\n\n**None of the review's findings required disagreement.** All five issues were verified as correct against the actual source code. The gaps were reasonable observations with appropriate severity levels. The review was thorough and well-calibrated — it didn't over-react to minor issues or miss major ones.\n\nThe only place where I deviated from the review's implicit suggestion was on the Vercel shallow clone issue (2.1). The review implied the plan should recommend a more robust command. I instead kept the simple command and added a caveat about the safe failure mode, because the simple approach is standard Vercel practice and the failure case (unnecessary build) is harmless.\n\n---\n\n## Net Effect on the Plan\n\nThe plan now has:\n- 1 additional file to modify (`tsconfig.json`)\n- 3 additional code changes in existing files (`release.ts` validation revert, `release.ts` git add, `test-sync-local.ts` rsync)\n- More precise instructions on field ordering, comments, and SOP prose\n- Better edge case documentation\n\nTotal modified files went from 7 to 8 existing files. Total lines of code changed went from ~25 to ~35. The migration remains small and focused — these additions prevent real breakages (especially the tsconfig exclude) that would have surfaced during the first CI run after migration.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:48:23.434Z"}
